Scalar entries commute, so $B_{jk}A_{ij}=A_{ij}B_{jk}$ is exactly the summed entry formula for <matrix multiplication>. Hence
$$
\boxed{C=AB}.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The repeated index sums the diagonal entries of $A$. This is its <trace>:
$$
\boxed{t=\operatorname{tr}A}.
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The equation is **not separable**, because $x^2-xy=x(x-y)$ cannot be written as a product of a function of $x$ and a function of $y$ on an open region. For example, the ratio $(x^2-xy_1)/(x^2-xy_2)=(x-y_1)/(x-y_2)$ depends on $x$ for distinct fixed $y_1,y_2$, whereas a separated product would give a constant ratio.

It is **not homogeneous** in the sense of a <homogeneous first-order differential equation>: the right-hand side is not a function of $y/x$ alone. It is also not a <homogeneous linear differential equation>, since moving the $xy$ term to the left leaves a nonzero forcing.

It is **linear**, since it has the standard <linear ordinary differential equation> form
$$
y'+xy=x^2.
$$
Thus the classifications are **(a) no, (b) no, (c) yes**.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The coefficient <matrix> has <determinant> $-1$, so it is an <invertible matrix>. Its <homogeneous linear system> therefore has only the zero solution:
$$
\boxed{x=y=z=0}.
$$
One can also subtract the first equation from the second to get $z=0$, then subtract the first from the third to get $y+2z=0$, giving $y=0$ and finally $x=0$. Thus **there are no nonzero solutions**.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <chain rule> gives $u_{xx}=f''(x+\lambda y)$ and $u_{yy}=\lambda^2f''(x+\lambda y)$. The <partial differential equation> becomes
$$
(4-\lambda^2)f''(x+\lambda y)=0.
$$
For this to hold for every twice <differentiable> $f$, the coefficient must vanish:
$$
\boxed{\lambda=2\quad\text{or}\quad\lambda=-2}.
$$
For an affine $f$ alone any $\lambda$ would work, but the requirement is for arbitrary $f$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <product-to-sum formula> gives the finite <Fourier series>
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac12\sin4x+\frac12\sin2x}.
$$
Thus the only nonzero <Fourier coefficients> are $b_2=b_4=1/2$.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Let $V$ be the enclosed region with piecewise smooth closed boundary $S$, oriented by its outward <unit normal> $\mathbf n$. For a continuously <differentiable> <vector field> on a neighbourhood of $V$, the <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\oiint_S\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n\,dS
=\iiint_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV}.
$$
The outward <flux integral> equals the <volume integral> of the <divergence>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Use the <Maclaurin series> $\sin3x=3x-\tfrac92x^3+O(x^5)$ and $\cos x=1-\tfrac12x^2+O(x^4)$. Multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=3x-6x^3+O(x^5)}.
$$
The same terms follow from the two sine harmonics in the <Fourier series> found above.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Apply <integration by parts> with $u=\log x$ and $dv=x\,dx$. Since $du=dx/x$ and $v=x^2/2$,
$$
\int x\log x\,dx=\frac{x^2}{2}\log x-\frac12\int x\,dx
=\boxed{\frac{x^2}{2}\log x-\frac{x^2}{4}+C},\qquad x>0.
$$
The original PDF specifies this <integral>; the local OCR replaces the entire question by unrelated text.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

First $f_x=2xy^3$ and $f_y=3x^2y^2$. Differentiating again gives the four second-order <partial derivatives>:
$$
\boxed{f_{xx}=2y^3,\qquad f_{xy}=6xy^2,\qquad f_{yx}=6xy^2,\qquad f_{yy}=6x^2y}.
$$
The two <mixed partial derivatives> agree, as expected for this smooth <polynomial>.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Three <vectors> form a <basis> of three-dimensional space exactly when their <scalar triple product> is nonzero:
$$
\boxed{\Delta=\mathbf u\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf w)\ne0}.
$$
It is the <determinant> of the <matrix> with these columns, so this condition is equivalent to <linear independence> and spanning the whole space.

Take the <dot product> of the expansion of $\mathbf y$ with $\mathbf v\times\mathbf w$. The terms containing $\mathbf v$ and $\mathbf w$ vanish, leaving $\mathbf y\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf w)=\alpha\Delta$. Cycling the same argument yields
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{\mathbf y\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf w)}{\Delta},\qquad
\beta=\frac{\mathbf y\cdot(\mathbf w\times\mathbf u)}{\Delta},\qquad
\gamma=\frac{\mathbf y\cdot(\mathbf u\times\mathbf v)}{\Delta}}.
$$
The three cross-product numerators use the <reciprocal basis>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the <cross product> with $\mathbf c$. Since $\mathbf c\times\mathbf c=0$, the equation implies
$$
\mathbf x\times\mathbf c=\mathbf a\times\mathbf c,
\qquad(\mathbf x-\mathbf a)\times\mathbf c=0.
$$
As $\mathbf c\ne0$, the displacement is parallel to $\mathbf c$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x=\mathbf a+\lambda\mathbf c,\qquad\lambda\in\mathbb R}.
$$
This is a necessary condition; substitution must still determine which points on this <straight line> satisfy the full equation.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Substitute $\mathbf x=\mathbf a+\lambda\mathbf c$ into the original equation and cancel $\mathbf a$. Because $\mathbf c$ is nonzero, the scalar condition is
$$
\boxed{\lambda(1-\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c)=\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a}.
$$
If $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c\ne1$, there is one value, $\lambda=(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a)/(1-\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c)$. If $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c=1$ and $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a=0$, every real $\lambda$ works. If $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c=1$ and $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a\ne0$, the scalar equation is inconsistent and there is no solution.

Thus “undefined” in the last case means that no parameter value solves the equation; it must not be used to conceal the distinct case with infinitely many solutions.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In the nonsingular case the unique solution is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x=\mathbf a+\frac{\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a}{1-\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c}\,\mathbf c,
\qquad \mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c\ne1}.
$$
Geometrically this is a single selected point of the <straight line> through $\mathbf a$ parallel to $\mathbf c$.

When $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c=1$ and $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a=0$, the solution set is that entire <straight line>, $\mathbf a+\lambda\mathbf c$. The remaining case has empty solution set. In <matrix> terms the equation is $(I-\mathbf c\mathbf b^T)\mathbf x=\mathbf a$, a <rank-one matrix update> of the <identity matrix>.

