= Solution
{parent=a}

The <gradient> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\bigl(y(3x^2+y^2-1),\ x(x^2+3y^2-1)\bigr)^T}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At $A=(1/2,1/4)$, the <gradient> is $(-3/64,-9/32)^T$. The position <vector> has length $\sqrt5/4$, so its normalized direction is $(2,1)^T/\sqrt5$. Hence the <directional derivative> is
$$
\boxed{D_{\hat{\mathbf a}}f(A)
=\frac{(2,1)}{\sqrt5}\cdot\left(-\frac3{64},-\frac9{32}\right)
=-\frac{3\sqrt5}{40}}.
$$

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a <stationary point>, the two factors from part (a) must vanish. If $y=0$, the second equation gives $x(x^2-1)=0$; if $x=0$, the first gives $y(y^2-1)=0$. Thus the axis points are $(0,0),(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)$.

If both coordinates are nonzero, solve $3x^2+y^2=1$ and $x^2+3y^2=1$, giving $x^2=y^2=1/4$. These four points complete the list of nine <stationary points>.

For classification, the <Hessian matrix> is
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}6xy&3x^2+3y^2-1\\3x^2+3y^2-1&6xy\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $(0,0)$ its <eigenvalues> are $1,-1$, and at the four unit-axis points they are $2,-2$. These five points are <saddle points of a scalar function>, all with value zero.

At $(1/2,1/2)$ and $(-1/2,-1/2)$ the <eigenvalues> are one and two, so both are strict <local minima> of value $-1/8$. At $(1/2,-1/2)$ and $(-1/2,1/2)$ they are minus one and minus two, so both are strict <local maxima> of value $1/8$. This follows from the <second-derivative test>. These extrema are local: the <polynomial> is unbounded above and below on the full <plane>.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The zero <level set> is especially simple:
$$
\boxed{f=0\iff x=0\quad\text{or}\quad y=0\quad\text{or}\quad x^2+y^2=1}.
$$
The two axes and the unit <circle> divide the <plane> into regions where the signs alternate. Inside the <circle>, $f$ has the opposite sign to $xy$; outside it has the same sign. Closed <level curves> surround the two interior <local minima> and two interior <local maxima>; the zero curves cross at the five <saddle points of a scalar function>. Farther out, the nonzero <level curves> are open within the displayed square.

\Image[paper-2-q12-contours.png]
{title=Contours, stationary points and gradient directions of the quartic surface}
{description=Left: signed <level curves> and the exact zero set consisting of both axes and the unit <circle>. Right: normalized <gradient> directions, including arrows on the zero curves. The nine <stationary points> are marked in both panels.}

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Near each <local minimum>, the <gradient> points outward; near each <local maximum>, it points inward. The five remaining points have saddle directions, determined by their indefinite <Hessian matrices>.

On the $x$-axis the exact <gradient> is $(0,x(x^2-1))^T$, and on the $y$-axis it is $(y(y^2-1),0)^T$. Thus arrows are perpendicular to the axes and reverse direction at the origin and the unit-axis <stationary points>.

On the unit <circle>, substitute $x^2+y^2=1$ into the <gradient> to obtain
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=2xy(x,y)^T}.
$$
It is radial outward in quadrants I and III, radial inward in quadrants II and IV, and zero at the four axis intersections. These arrows, together with the nearby saddle and extremum patterns, are shown in the right panel of the contour figure. The <gradient> is normal to every regular <level curve> and points toward increasing values of $f$.

