With $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t^2)^T$, the <tangent vector> is $(1,2t)^T$ and $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(t^3,2t^2)^T$. The <line integral> is
$$
\int_0^1(t^3+4t^3)\,dt=\boxed{\frac54}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\mathbf r(t)=(t^2,t)^T$, the <tangent vector> is $(2t,1)^T$ and $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(t^3,t^4+t)^T$. Thus
$$
\int_0^1(2t^4+t^4+t)\,dt=\frac35+\frac12=\boxed{\frac{11}{10}}.
$$
The unequal values for the same endpoints already rule out a <conservative vector field>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $q=2t-t^2$. The second coordinate is exactly $q^2$, so this is the same parabola as in part (i), with a different increasing parametrization. Since $q$ runs from zero to one and $dq=(2-2t)dt$, the <line integral> is
$$
\int_0^1 5q^3\,dq=\boxed{\frac54}.
$$
Equivalently, substitute the original parameter to get $\int_0^15(2t-t^2)^3(2-2t)dt$. This demonstrates that a <line integral> depends on the oriented path, not on its speed of traversal.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <curl> of $\mathbf G=\mathbf a\times\mathbf r$ is $2\mathbf a$. Thus for $\mathbf a\ne0$ it cannot be a <gradient field>, while for $\mathbf a=0$ it is zero and all its <vector-field potentials> are constants. This zero-vector case is allowed by the question.

For the radial <vector field> $\mathbf H$, write $\rho=|\mathbf r|$. Choose a radial primitive
$$
\Phi(\rho)=\int_{\rho_0}^{\rho}s f(s)\,ds+C.
$$
For $\rho>0$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\nabla\Phi=\Phi'(\rho)\frac{\mathbf r}{\rho}=f(\rho)\mathbf r.
$$
Hence all <vector-field potentials> on a connected domain where the field is defined are
$$
\boxed{\Phi(\mathbf r)=\int_{\rho_0}^{|\mathbf r|}s f(s)\,ds+C}.
$$
The assumed existence of the needed derivatives and integrals justifies this construction. If the domain includes the origin and the field is regular there, choose $\rho_0=0$ and extend the primitive there. Any two <vector-field potentials> differ by a constant on each connected component.

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $N_L$ count successful lemon seeds. By <independence> it has a <binomial distribution> with five trials and success <probability> $y$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N_L=2)=\binom52y^2(1-y)^3=10y^2(1-y)^3}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the <complement of an event>: fewer than two successes means zero or one. These <mutually exclusive events> have <probabilities> $(1-y)^5$ and $5y(1-y)^4$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N_L\geq2)=1-(1-y)^5-5y(1-y)^4}.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $N_A$ be the independent avocado count. There are three disjoint ways to have fewer than two total trees: no trees, only one avocado, or only one lemon. Their <probabilities> give
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb P(N_A+N_L\geq2)=1
&-(1-x)^5(1-y)^5\\
&-5x(1-x)^4(1-y)^5\\
&-5y(1-y)^4(1-x)^5.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The sum is generally a <Poisson binomial distribution>, rather than a single <binomial distribution>, because the two success <probabilities> may differ.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

There are three possible count pairs conditional on two trees. Their <probabilities>, from the two independent <binomial distributions>, are
$$
p_{20}=10x^2(1-x)^3(1-y)^5,\qquad
p_{02}=10y^2(1-y)^3(1-x)^5,
$$
$$
p_{11}=25xy(1-x)^4(1-y)^4.
$$
The first two correspond to matching kinds, so the <conditional probability> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{p_{20}+p_{02}}{p_{20}+p_{02}+p_{11}}}.
$$
This form remains valid at endpoints whenever the conditioning event has positive <probability>; if the denominator vanishes, the <conditional probability> is undefined. As a check, when $x=y\in(0,1)$ the result is $20/45=4/9$, the fraction of two-element subsets containing matching kinds.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Each tree independently has ten equally likely integer yields. Of the $100$ ordered pairs, exactly $(30,32),(31,31),(32,30)$ sum to sixty-two. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(62\text{ seeds})=\frac3{100}}.
$$
The <uniform distribution on a finite set> applies to the individual yields, not to their sum.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $n$ independent seeds the <probability> of at least one success is $1-(1-x)^n$. Requiring it to be at least $0.95$ gives $(1-x)^n\leq0.05$. For $0<x<1$, taking logarithms and dividing by the negative number $\log(1-x)$ reverses the inequality:
$$
n\geq\frac{\log0.05}{\log(1-x)}.
$$
The smallest permitted integer is therefore
$$
\boxed{n_{\min}=\left\lceil\frac{\log0.05}{\log(1-x)}\right\rceil}.
$$
The <ceiling function> is necessary because seeds are counted discretely. If $x=0$, no finite number suffices; if $x=1$, one seed suffices. The target is interpreted as at least a ninety-five percent chance, since exact equality may not be attainable with an integer count.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On a nonconstant branch with $\sin y\ne0$, regard $x$ as a function of $y$. The equation becomes the <linear ordinary differential equation>
$$
\frac{dx}{dy}+x\cot y=2\cos y.
$$
An <integrating factor> is $\sin y$, giving
$$
\frac d{dy}(x\sin y)=2\sin y\cos y.
$$
Taking an <antiderivative> yields the implicit family
$$
\boxed{x\sin y-\sin^2y=C}.
$$
On a regular branch this can also be written $x=\sin y+C/\sin y$. The latter form loses the constant solutions $y=n\pi$, which must be restored separately.

The implicit relation is a <first integral of an ordinary differential equation>: along any differentiable solution of the original equation,
$$
\frac d{dx}(x\sin y-\sin^2y)
=\sin y-(2\sin y-x)\cos y\,y'=0.
$$
Conversely, a differentiable graph satisfying this relation solves the original equation wherever $\cos y\ne0$. The original equation is undefined when $\cos y=0$; points at which $2\sin y-x=0$ also need separate attention before solving explicitly for $y'$. This is why dividing immediately by $\tan y$ can lose valid solutions and obscure nonuniqueness.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The initial data force $C=0$, so the implicit relation factors as $\sin y(x-\sin y)=0$. Near $(0,0)$ this permits either the constant branch or the sine branch:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=0\quad\text{or}\quad y(x)=\arcsin x\quad\text{locally at }x=0}.
$$
Both satisfy the original equation, including its degenerate value $0=0$ at the initial point. They have different slopes there, zero and one, so the <initial value problem> is not unique. There are no further differentiable local branches through that point: away from $x=0$ the two factors cannot switch, and joining the two different branches at zero would give unequal one-sided <derivatives>.

The nonconstant branch is defined as a solution for $-1<x<1$; the constant branch exists for every real $x$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Again $C=0$. Since the initial sine is nonzero, the local branch must satisfy $\sin y=x$. Selecting the inverse-sine branch through $y=\pi/6$ gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\arcsin x,\qquad -1<x<1}.
$$
This passes through the specified point and has $y'=1/\sqrt{1-x^2}$, which verifies the original equation on this interval.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Here $C=0$ as well, but the branch through $5\pi/6$ is the other inverse-sine branch:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\pi-\arcsin x,\qquad -1<x<1}.
$$
Its <derivative> is $-1/\sqrt{1-x^2}$, and both the sign of $\tan y$ and the original equation agree with this choice.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <characteristic equation> of the associated <homogeneous linear differential equation> is $m^2+7m+12=(m+3)(m+4)=0$. A constant <particular integral> is $1/2$, so
$$
y=\frac12+Ae^{-3x}+Be^{-4x}.
$$
The first <boundary condition> gives $A+B=-1/2$. The second gives $Ae^{-1}+Be^{-4/3}=-e^{-1}/2$. Eliminating $A$ yields $B(e^{-4/3}-e^{-1})=0$, hence $B=0$ and $A=-1/2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{1-e^{-3x}}2,\qquad y(1)=\frac{1-e^{-3}}2}.
$$

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use cylindrical radius $\rho=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. The upper <cone> is $z=1-\rho$ and the lower <elliptic paraboloid> is $z=\rho^2-1$, both with $0\leq\rho\leq1$. They meet along the unit <circle> in $z=0$. The enclosed solid is
$$
0\leq\rho\leq1,\qquad \rho^2-1\leq z\leq1-\rho.
$$
Thus the coordinate ranges on the complete surface are
$$
\boxed{-1\leq x\leq1,\qquad-1\leq y\leq1,\qquad-1\leq z\leq1}.
$$
These separate ranges do not mean every point of their Cartesian product lies on the surface. A vertical cross-section has a straight upper cone profile and a curved lower paraboloid profile.

\Image[paper-2-q16-shape.png]
{title=A cone and a paraboloid joined along their unit-circle rim}
{description=The upper <cone> has apex $(0,0,1)$; the lower <elliptic paraboloid> has vertex $(0,0,-1)$. Both meet at cylindrical radius one and height zero. The meridian cross-section shows the enclosed vertical range at each radius.}

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Both patches project onto the unit <disk> $D$ in the $xy$-<plane>. Their outward <oriented surface elements> are
$$
d\mathbf S_+=\left(\frac{x}{\rho},\frac{y}{\rho},1\right)^Tdx\,dy,
\qquad d\mathbf S_-=(2x,2y,-1)^Tdx\,dy.
$$
The cone's apex has zero <area> and does not affect the <surface integral>. For $k=0$, the <vector field> is $(y,x,1)^T$, so
$$
\Phi_+=\iint_D\left(\frac{2xy}{\rho}+1\right)dx\,dy=\pi,
\qquad
\Phi_-=\iint_D(4xy-1)dx\,dy=-\pi.
$$
The terms involving $xy$ vanish by reflection symmetry of the <disk>. Thus the total outward <flux> is
$$
\boxed{\Phi=0}.
$$
This calculation evaluates the two <surface integrals> directly.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $k=1$, the <vector field> is $(x^2y,xy^2,z)^T$. On the cone,
$$
\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S_+
=\left(\frac{x^3y+xy^3}{\rho}+1-\rho\right)dx\,dy
=(xy\rho+1-\rho)dx\,dy.
$$
The first term has zero <integral> by symmetry. Using <polar coordinates> for the remaining <double integral> gives
$$
\Phi_+=2\pi\int_0^1(1-\rho)\rho\,d\rho=\frac\pi3.
$$
On the paraboloid $z=\rho^2-1$,
$$
\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S_-
=\bigl(2x^3y+2xy^3-(\rho^2-1)\bigr)dx\,dy
=(2xy\rho^2+1-\rho^2)dx\,dy.
$$
Again the $xy$ term cancels by symmetry, giving
$$
\Phi_-=2\pi\int_0^1(1-\rho^2)\rho\,d\rho=\frac\pi2.
$$
Therefore direct <surface integral> yields
$$
\boxed{\Phi=\frac\pi3+\frac\pi2=\frac{5\pi}{6}}.
$$
No separate disk at $z=0$ is included: that rim joins the two surfaces and is not an additional face of the closed boundary.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The displayed <matrix> is **not orthogonal**: its first column has squared <Euclidean norm> three, whereas every column of an <orthogonal matrix> must have squared length one.

For the general assertion, put $C=AB$. In the <Einstein summation convention>, orthogonality gives $A_{ki}A_{kj}=\delta_{ij}$ and $B_{ki}B_{kj}=\delta_{ij}$. The entries of $C$ satisfy
$$
C_{ki}C_{kj}
=A_{k\ell}B_{\ell i}A_{km}B_{mj}
=(A_{k\ell}A_{km})B_{\ell i}B_{mj}
=\delta_{\ell m}B_{\ell i}B_{mj}
=B_{\ell i}B_{\ell j}=\delta_{ij}.
$$
This is exactly $C^TC=I$, proving that the <matrix product> $AB$ is orthogonal. The <Kronecker delta> contracts the two middle indices without changing the multiplication order of the <matrices>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <matrix> form is
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\1&1&a-1\\a&2&3\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\3\end{pmatrix}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract the first row from the third. Expansion along the resulting row $(a-1,0,0)$ gives the <determinant>
$$
\det A=(a-1)\det\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\1&a-1\end{pmatrix}
=\boxed{(a-1)(2a-5)}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\det A=0\iff a=1\text{ or }a=\frac52}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $a\ne1,5/2$, the <matrix> is invertible, so there is exactly one solution. To exhibit it, subtract the first <linear equation> from the third to obtain $(a-1)x=2$, then subtract twice the second from the first to obtain $-x+(5-2a)z=-3$. Back-substitution gives
$$
\boxed{x=\frac2{a-1},\qquad
z=\frac{3a-5}{(a-1)(2a-5)},\qquad
y=\frac{a^2-10a+15}{(a-1)(2a-5)}}.
$$
At $a=1$, the first and third <linear equations> have identical left-hand sides but right-hand sides one and three, so there is no solution.

At $a=5/2$, subtracting the first from the third forces $x=4/3$, while the first minus twice the second forces $x=3$. These contradict each other, so there is again no solution. Thus there is **one solution for $a\ne1,5/2$, no solution for either exceptional value, and no parameter value with infinitely many solutions**.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A standard complete orthogonal family is the real <Fourier basis>
$$
\boxed{1,\ \cos x,\ \sin x,\ \cos2x,\ \sin2x,\ldots}.
$$
Completeness gives expansion in the <L2 space> for a square-integrable <function>. Mere integrability alone does not guarantee pointwise convergence of a <Fourier series>; the explicit function in part (b) is continuous and piecewise smooth, so this issue does not obstruct its expansion.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With <inner product> $\langle u,v\rangle=\int_{-\pi}^\pi u(x)v(x)dx$, <orthogonality> means
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\pi}^\pi e_n(x)e_m(x)\,dx=0\quad(m\ne n)}.
$$
The constant function has squared <norm> $2\pi$, and each sine or cosine has squared <norm> $\pi$. Thus the displayed family is orthogonal but not normalized; these different lengths determine the coefficient factors.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The standard <Fourier series> is
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty(a_n\cos nx+b_n\sin nx),
$$
with <Fourier coefficients>
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^\pi f(x)\cos nx\,dx\quad(n\geq0),\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^\pi f(x)\sin nx\,dx\quad(n\geq1)}.
$$
These follow by taking <inner products> with the individual <basis vectors> and using their <orthogonality> and squared <norms>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Separate the already finite sine terms from the even part $g(x)=x^2-\pi|x|$. Since $g$ is an <even function>, it has no sine coefficients. Its constant coefficient is
$$
a_0=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi(x^2-\pi x)dx=-\frac{\pi^2}{3}.
$$
For $n\geq1$, <integration by parts> gives
$$
\int_0^\pi x\cos nx\,dx=\frac{(-1)^n-1}{n^2},\qquad
\int_0^\pi x^2\cos nx\,dx=\frac{2\pi(-1)^n}{n^2}.
$$
Therefore the cosine <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
a_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi(x^2-\pi x)\cos nx\,dx
=\frac{2(1+(-1)^n)}{n^2}.
$$
Only even frequencies survive: $a_{2m}=1/m^2$. The sine <Fourier coefficients> are $b_1=1$, $b_2=-2$, with all others zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sin x-2\sin2x-\frac{\pi^2}{6}
+\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac{\cos2mx}{m^2}}.
$$
The periodic function is continuous at zero and at its endpoint joins, and its coefficients are absolutely summable. Thus this <Fourier series> represents it everywhere, including those joins.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Evaluate at $x=\pi/2$, where the original expression gives $f(\pi/2)=1-\pi^2/4$. The <Fourier series> gives
$$
1-\frac{\pi^2}{4}=1-\frac{\pi^2}{6}
+\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^m}{m^2}.
$$
Rearranging proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^m}{m^2}=-\frac{\pi^2}{12}}.
$$
The convergence at this point is justified by the continuity properties noted in part (b).

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a regular constrained extremum, every <tangent vector> to the <level set> $g=A$ gives zero <directional derivative> of $f$. Thus $\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the tangent <plane>, and must be a scalar multiple of its nonzero <normal vector> $\nabla g$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g,\qquad g=A}.
$$
One solves these four equations for $x,y,z,\lambda$, evaluates $f$ at the candidates, and classifies or compares the values as required. Points where $\nabla g=0$, and any boundary or limiting cases of the feasible domain, require separate checks because the regularity argument does not cover them.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $g=x^2+y^2+z^2$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
y=2\lambda x,\qquad x+z=2\lambda y,\qquad y=2\lambda z,
\qquad x^2+y^2+z^2=1.
$$
Taking the <dot product> with $(x,y,z)^T$ gives $2T=2\lambda$, so $\lambda=T$.

If $\lambda\ne0$, the first and third equations give $x=z$. The middle equation then gives $x=\lambda y$, so $2\lambda^2=1$. The norm constraint gives $x^2=z^2=1/4$ and $y^2=1/2$. Thus the hottest points and their temperature are
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\frac\varepsilon2,\frac\varepsilon{\sqrt2},\frac\varepsilon2\right),\quad
T_{\max}=\frac1{\sqrt2},\quad\varepsilon\in\{-1,1\}}.
$$
The coldest points are
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\frac\varepsilon2,-\frac\varepsilon{\sqrt2},\frac\varepsilon2\right),\quad
T_{\min}=-\frac1{\sqrt2},\quad\varepsilon\in\{-1,1\}}.
$$
For $\lambda=0$, the equations give $y=0$, $z=-x$, and hence the other two constrained <stationary points> are
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(\frac\varepsilon{\sqrt2},0,-\frac\varepsilon{\sqrt2}\right),\qquad T=0}.
$$
They are saddle points of the restriction to the <sphere>: with $u=(x+z)/\sqrt2$ and $v=y$ as local tangent coordinates there, $T=\sqrt2uv$ changes sign in every neighbourhood. The surface is compact and smooth, so the listed extreme temperatures are global. Here “zero gradient on the surface” means zero <surface gradient>; the ambient <gradient> at the hottest and coldest points is normal to the <sphere>, rather than zero.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Introduce the second constraint $q(x,y,z)=y-h(x)=0$ and a second <Lagrange multiplier> $\mu$. At points where the two constraint <gradients> are <linearly independent>, solve
$$
\boxed{(y,x+z,y)^T
=\lambda(2x,2y,2z)^T+\mu(-h'(x),1,0)^T},
$$
$$
\boxed{x^2+y^2+z^2=1,\qquad y=h(x)}.
$$
These are five scalar equations for $x,y,z,\lambda,\mu$. The two constraint <normal vectors> span the normal <plane> to the feasible curve. Points where those <gradients> become dependent must be examined separately; the regular two-multiplier condition need not cover them.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a separated nonzero solution $T=f(x)g(y)$, substitution and division by $fg$ gives
$$
a\frac{f'}f=\frac{g''}g=-\lambda^2.
$$
The homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> impose $g(0)=g(1)=0$. For a nonzero spatial factor, <integration by parts> gives $\lambda^2\int_0^1g^2dy=\int_0^1(g')^2dy$, so the separation value is nonnegative. The zero value gives only a trivial linear $g$ under these boundary conditions. The nontrivial separated factors, for $a\ne0$, are therefore
$$
\boxed{g_n(y)=B_n\sin(n\pi y),\qquad
f_n(x)=A_n e^{-n^2\pi^2x/a},\qquad n=1,2,\ldots}.
$$
For the intended forward <diffusion equation>, assume $a>0$. The <superposition principle> gives a <Fourier sine series>, whose coefficients must expand the initial ramp in the interior:
$$
b_n=2\int_0^1y\sin(n\pi y)dy=\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\pi}.
$$
Consequently the standard heat-flow solution is
$$
\boxed{T(x,y)=\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty
\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\,e^{-n^2\pi^2x/a}\sin(n\pi y),\qquad a>0,\ x>0}.
$$
It satisfies both lateral <boundary conditions> for $x>0$, and tends to $y$ as $x\downarrow0$ for $0<y<1$, also converging to the ramp in the <L2 norm>. The exponential coefficient decay justifies differentiating the series for positive $x$.

There is a genuine incompatibility in the printed data: at $(0,1)$ the lateral condition demands zero while the initial condition demands one. Thus **no function satisfies every stated value at that corner**. The displayed series solves the usual consistent interpretation with initial data on $0<y<1$ and lateral data for $x>0$; it cannot be continuous at the upper initial corner. This discrepancy is present in the original PDF.

The sign of $a$ is also unstated. If $a=0$, the spatial equation with two zero endpoints forces $T=0$, incompatible with the ramp. If $a<0$, the separated Fourier coefficients grow like $e^{n^2\pi^2x/|a|}/n$ and fail to be square summable for every $x>0$; these data therefore have no forward solution in the standard <L2 space> class. The positive-$a$ assumption is essential to the stated heat-flow interpretation.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $t>0$, differentiate the printed ansatz:
$$
h=a t^{-1/3}-\frac{ax^2}{t}-bt,\qquad
h_t=-\frac a3t^{-4/3}+\frac{ax^2}{t^2}-b,
$$
$$
h_x=-\frac{2ax}{t},\qquad
(hh_x)_x=6a^2\frac{x^2}{t^2}-2a^2t^{-4/3}+2ab.
$$
Matching the independent coefficients in $h_t=(hh_x)_x-1$ gives
$$
a=6a^2,\qquad \frac a3=2a^2,\qquad b(1+2a)=1.
$$
There are two parameter pairs, including the degenerate spatially constant case:
$$
\boxed{(a,b)=\left(\frac16,\frac34\right)\quad\text{or}\quad(a,b)=(0,1)}.
$$
Their corresponding solutions are
$$
\boxed{h(x,t)=\frac1{6t^{1/3}}-\frac{x^2}{6t}-\frac34t}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\boxed{h(x,t)=-t}.
$$
The request for “the general form” cannot mean the complete solution set of this nonlinear <partial differential equation>: substitution of one ansatz cannot provide arbitrary initial data, and the <superposition principle> does not apply.

A useful wider family follows by allowing an independent time-dependent constant term and quadratic coefficient, $h=A(t)+B(t)x^2$. Coefficient comparison gives $B'=6B^2$ and $A'=2AB-1$. For $B\ne0$, write $\tau=t-t_0>0$ to obtain $B=-1/(6\tau)$ and $A=C\tau^{-1/3}-3\tau/4$. Spatial translation, also a symmetry of the equation, therefore gives
$$
\boxed{h(x,t)=C\tau^{-1/3}-\frac{(x-x_0)^2}{6\tau}-\frac34\tau,
\qquad\tau=t-t_0>0}.
$$
Here $C,x_0,t_0$ are arbitrary constants; the displayed nontrivial ansatz corresponds to $C=1/6$ and $x_0=t_0=0$. There are also affine solutions $h=px+(p^2-1)t+q$, including the spatially constant branch. These are valid solution families, rather than a claim of a full general solution.
